Z||000000||JohnnyK||11-02-2001||07:33 PM||||Ok, my dad brought home a DVD player about a month ago, and I've played about 2-3 DVD's in it. Now all of a sudden, I go to turn it on, and it won't. thought it might have been the fuse so I replaced it, but it blew instantly. The problem is, he bought it in Florida, and I live in Toronto, and the closest Toshiba place is Markham, but they won't take it because it's from the states, and they say I have to mail it down there. To further complicate the dilemma, there is a rental DVD stuck in it that was due back a few days ago! Argh.. any suggestions?||||reg||1
Z||000001||maryjane||11-02-2001||07:52 PM||||Well, no suggestions, other than dis-assembly. Then the warranty will probably be voided. Your luck reminds me of the old 'lost dog' ad.<BR>Blind in one eye, tail missing, broken right rear leg, piece missing from left ear, deaf, castrated. Goes by name "lucky".  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Good luck with it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||11-02-2001||08:11 PM||||You should talk to somebody in charge.Why should you have to send their product to another country to get it fixed.Maybe you talked to somebody that didn't know what they were talking about or were to be the one to fix it and they were lazy.Don't take no for an answer.Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cheever3000||11-03-2001||12:00 AM||||Maybe there is a repair shop which may not be "recognized" by Toshiba, but they can fix them anyway.  Call around.  Ask "if you don't work on them, do you know who does".<P>Why buy junk American crap?  Everybody knows all the good stuff is made in Japan & China.<BR>||||reg||1
